      "kind": "title",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Sparse Attention for Long Scientific Documents"
        }
      ],
      "align": "center"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Ada Lovelace, Charles Babbage, and Mary Somerville"
        }
      ],
      "align": "center",
      "font": {
        "shape": "serif",
        "size": 10.96
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "footnote",
      "label": "",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Manuscript received June 1, 2026; revised August 3, 2026."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "footnote",
      "label": "",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "A. Lovelace and C. Babbage are with the Department of Computing, Analytical Engines Ltd., London, UK (e-mail: ada@example.org)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "footnote",
      "label": "",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "M. Somerville is with the School of Mathematics, Northfield University, Northfield, UK."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Abstract—",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Transformers read long documents slowly because attention compares every pair of tokens. We study a sparse pattern in which each token attends to a window of its neighbors and to a small set of global tokens. On two benchmarks of scientific articles the model matches or exceeds dense attention while using a quarter of the memory, and an ablation shows that 64 global tokens capture most of the gain.",
          "bold": true
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify",
      "font": {
        "shape": "serif",
        "size": 9.96,
        "bold": true
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Index Terms—",
          "bold": true,
          "italic": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Attention, long documents, sparsity, summarization, transformers.",
          "bold": true
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify",
      "font": {
        "shape": "serif",
        "size": 9.96,
        "bold": true
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "I. "
        },
        {
          "text": "INTRODUCTION",
          "smallCaps": true
        }
      ],
      "align": "center"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "SCIENTIFIC articles run to thousands of tokens, and their key results often sit far from the questions they answer. A model that summarizes an article has to connect a claim in the abstract with the table that supports it on page nine. Dense attention [1] does this directly, but its cost grows with the square of the length, so most systems truncate the input to a few thousand tokens."
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify",
      "indent": "first"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Sparse attention lowers the cost by letting each token attend to only some positions [2], [3]. The pattern matters: a window captures local context, and a few global tokens carry information across the whole document."
        },
        {
          "text": "1",
          "sup": true
        },
        {
          "text": " This paper measures how far such a pattern goes on scientific text, where long-range references are common."
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify",
      "indent": "first"
    },
    {
      "kind": "footnote",
      "label": "1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Code and trained models will be released with the camera-ready version."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Our contributions are threefold. First, we describe a window-and-global pattern with a simple implementation. Second, we evaluate it on two benchmarks of long articles. Third, we show which parts of the pattern account for the gain."
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify",
      "indent": "first"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "II. "
        },
        {
          "text": "RELATED WORK",
          "smallCaps": true
        }
      ],
      "align": "center"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "A. Efficient attention",
          "italic": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Several families of methods reduce the cost of attention. Kernel methods approximate the softmax with random features, low-rank methods project the keys to a shorter sequence, and sparse methods restrict the pairs that interact [2]–[4]. We follow the last family, since it keeps exact attention within the chosen pairs."
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify",
      "indent": "first"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "B. Long-document summarization",
          "italic": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Benchmarks built from arXiv and PubMed articles pair each paper with its abstract [5]. Their inputs average more than 5,000 tokens, which makes them a natural test for long-context models."
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify",
      "indent": "first"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "III. "
        },
        {
          "text": "METHOD",
          "smallCaps": true
        }
      ],
      "align": "center"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Given queries "
        },
        {
          "text": "Q",
          "latex": "Q"
        },
        {
          "text": ", keys "
        },
        {
          "text": "K",
          "latex": "K"
        },
        {
          "text": ", and values "
        },
        {
          "text": "V",
          "latex": "V"
        },
        {
          "text": " for a sequence of length "
        },
        {
          "text": "n",
          "latex": "n"
        },
        {
          "text": ", dense attention computes"
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify",
      "indent": "first"
    },
    {
      "kind": "equation",
      "latex": "\\operatorname{Attn}(Q,K,V)=\\operatorname{softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d}}\\right)V,",
      "label": "(1)"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "where "
        },
        {
          "text": "d",
          "latex": "d"
        },
        {
          "text": " is the head dimension. Sparse attention replaces the score matrix with"
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify"
    },
    {
      "kind": "equation",
      "latex": "S_{ij}=\\begin{cases}q_i^{\\top}k_j/\\sqrt{d}, & j\\in\\mathcal{N}(i),\\\\ -\\infty, & \\text{otherwise},\\end{cases}",
      "label": "(2)"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "so that token "
        },
        {
          "text": "i",
          "latex": "i"
        },
        {
          "text": " attends only to its neighborhood"
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify"
    },
    {
      "kind": "equation",
      "latex": "\\mathcal{N}(i)=\\{j:|i-j|\\le w\\}\\cup\\mathcal{G},",
      "label": "(3)"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "a window of half-width "
        },
        {
          "text": "w",
          "latex": "w"
        },
        {
          "text": " together with the set "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "latex": "\\mathcal{G}"
        },
        {
          "text": " of global positions. Global tokens attend to every position in both directions."
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "1) Implementation:",
          "italic": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The pattern needs no custom kernel. Listing 1 builds the mask from a band and a set of global rows and columns, and applies it before the softmax."
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify"
    },
    {
      "kind": "code",
      "text": "def sparse_attention(q, k, v, window, global_idx):\n    scores = q @ k.T / q.shape[-1] ** 0.5\n    mask = band(len(q), window)\n    mask[global_idx, :] = True\n    mask[:, global_idx] = True\n    scores[~mask] = float(\"-inf\")\n    return softmax(scores) @ v"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "2) Training objective:",
          "italic": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "We train with the usual token-level cross-entropy,"
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify"
    },
    {
      "kind": "equation",
      "latex": "\\mathcal{L}=-\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\log p_\\theta(y_t\\mid y_{<t},x),",
      "label": "(4)"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "where "
        },
        {
          "text": "x",
          "latex": "x"
        },
        {
          "text": " is the article and "
        },
        {
          "text": "y",
          "latex": "y"
        },
        {
          "text": " its abstract. Fig. 1 shows the whole model."
        }
      ],
      "align": "justify"
    },
    {
      "kind": "figure",
      "caption": [
        {
          "text": "Fig. 1. Overview of the model. Global tokens attend to every position; all other tokens attend within a window."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
